- How would you evolve the schema when new fields are introduced while preserving backward compatibility?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a junior Azure developer's ability to design multi-tenant APIs with API versioning, schema-driven validation, and environment-aware configuration. It also tests understanding of data isolation patterns and CI/CD pipeline testing strategies.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant data isolation using tenantId as the partition key
- API versioning via the X-API-VERSION request header
- Per-version schema storage in Azure App Configuration
- Version-aware data routing and schema-driven payload validation
- End-to-end testing across dev, stage, and production environments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Version routing sketch
function routeRequest(req) {
  const version = req.headers['x-api-version'] || 'v1';
  const schema = appConfig.get(`schemas.${version}`);

  if (!schema) {
    return { status: 400, body: 'Unsupported API version' };
  }

  const validated = validatePayload(req.body, schema);
  if (!validated.valid) {
    return { status: 422, body: validated.errors };
  }

  return { version, partitionKey: req.headers['tenant-id'] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of idempotence, per-user isolation, and basic orchestration in Azure Functions using Durable Functions.

## Key Concepts
- Durable Functions orchestrator and fan-out/fan-in
- Idempotent processing using a stable id (blob name)
- Cosmos DB upsert with per-user partitioning
- Service Bus message deduplication via MessageId

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-durable orchestrator sketch
const df = require('durable-functions');
module.exports = df.app();
async function orchestrator(context) {
  const blobName = context.bindingData.name;
  const imageId = blobName; // dedupe key
  // check existing in Cosmos, then upsert
  // fan-out to Resize activities
  const outputs = await context.df.Task.all([
    context.df.callActivity('ResizeA', {imageId}),
    context.df.callActivity('ResizeB', {imageId}),
    context.df.callActivity('ResizeC', {imageId})
  ]);
  await context.df.callActivity('UpsertMetadata', {imageId, userId: context.bindingData.userId});
  await context.df.callActivity('PublishStatus', {imageId});
  return outputs;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures during the fan-out?
- How would you test idempotency across replays without corrupting data?

